{
  "image_name": "3aca2c078639e42532d21434ee7a573e.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how transformed epithelial cells can remodel a normal tissue environment into one that supports tumor growth. At the top, altered epithelial cells release lipid mediators, labeled as prostaglandins and leukotrienes, which act through autocrine and paracrine signaling. These signals recruit or influence surrounding stromal and immune cells, including neutrophils, macrophages/monocytes, lymphocytes, natural killer cells, dendritic cells, and endothelial cells.\n\nThe central message is that these interacting cell types begin to generate inflammatory mediators, angiogenic factors, and growth factors, creating a feedback loop between the transformed cells and their microenvironment. This shifts the tissue from a “normal microenvironment” to a “tumour-supporting microenvironment.” In this supportive state, the same broad classes of cells are still present, but they now appear to promote rather than restrain disease.\n\nAt the bottom, the consequence of this altered microenvironment is summarized as increased proliferation, survival, migration and invasion of tumor cells, along with angiogenesis and immunosuppression. The final image shows expansion of disorganized epithelial cells into a growing lesion, emphasizing that inflammation-associated signaling and stromal reprogramming drive cancer progression.",
